<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crémieux Decree</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crémieux Decree was a decree issued by the French Government of National Defence on 24 October 1870, named after the justice minister Adolphe Crémieux. It granted French citizenship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, while Muslim Algerians remained subjects without full citizenship, a distinction that deepened divisions in the colony. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime revoked it in 1940, and it was restored in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cremieux-decree.jpg" alt="Crémieux Decree" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Profburp at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cremieux_136.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1830-1834, and a sénatus-consulte of 1865 allowed indigenous Algerians to apply for French citizenship, though few did. French officials believed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Algeria, many of whom had Sephardic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> relatives in France, would assimilate more readily than their neighbours. France had already given citizenship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> of France in 1790 and to the Ashkenazi Jews in September 1791. The aim in Algeria was the rapid acculturation of Algerian Jews into French Jews loyal to France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Decree 136 of 1870 declared the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Algeria French citizens, so that their civil and personal status would be settled by French law. It was signed by Adolphe Crémieux, Léon Gambetta as Minister of the Interior and Léon Martin Fourichon as Minister of the Navy and the Colonies, members of the provisional government then sitting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was besieged. It made about 35,000 people citizens. Decree 137 confirmed that Algerian Muslims were not French citizens, a rule fixed in 1875 in the Native code. The Berber Mozabite Jews were also left out and gained citizenship only in 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Within a generation most Algerian Jews spoke French and had embraced French culture. Many French colonists refused to accept Jews as citizens, and a wave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> followed that continued to worsen well into the mid-1900s. Muslim Algerians could in principle apply individually for citizenship, but only by renouncing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and its laws, and few requests were accepted.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>, the Interior Minister Marcel Peyrouton abolished the decree on 7 October 1940. After the Anglo-American landings of November 1942 and the assassination of Admiral François Darlan, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> reinstated it on 14 March 1943. It remained in force until Algerian independence in 1962, when more than 80 per cent of Algerian Jews, about 110,000 of some 130,000, left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crémieux Decree” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Algerian Jews, citizens since the <b>Crémieux Decree</b> of 1870, formed a third community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
